{id:"terrace-farm",name:"Terraced Harvest Hall",emoji:"🌾",tags:["Farm","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"8 hr",size:"27×22",photos:[P12],pr:[1.166],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A ring-shaped farm hall built around a terraced garden courtyard: log pillars and lantern-lit wings, a wheat field and irrigation channel on the roof, a staircase up to it, stone-rimmed crop beds with a scarecrow, two ponds and hedged borders.",steps:[["Lay the stone border","On flat ground, mark out a rectangle 27 blocks wide and 19 deep. Trace its edge with stone brick slabs, bottom half, one block wide, all the way round. Leave a gap of 3 blocks in the middle of one of the long edges: this is the front, and the gap is where the grand stairs will go. That is 85 slabs. The border will carry the hedge later.",[]],
["Lay the ground floor","Fill the space inside the border with oak planks, 25 blocks wide and 17 deep, on the same layer as the slabs. This is the floor of the ground-floor hall. That is 425 planks.",[]],
["Stand the outer pillars","The building is a grid of pillars, 7 columns across and 5 rows deep, every fourth block, starting on the first block of the floor. First stand the outer ones: oak logs 3 tall (layers 2–4) on the four corners, then every fourth block along each edge: 7 along the back, 7 along the front, and 3 more on each side between the corners. That is 20 pillars, 60 logs.",[]],
["Stand the inner pillars","Fill in the rest of the grid inside: 5 columns by 3 rows, every fourth block, each 3 oak logs tall (layers 2–4). They hold up the deck above. That is 15 pillars, 45 logs.",[]],
["Wall the back and sides","Between the pillars there are gaps 3 blocks wide, called bays. On layer 2, fill every bay along the back wall (6 bays) and both side walls (4 bays each) with oak planks. Leave the front open, because it is an arcade. That is 14 bays, 42 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Above each wall plank, put 2 glass panes (layers 3–4), so every back and side bay is a tall window. That is 84 panes.",[]],
["Rail the front arcade","Along the open front, fill each of the 6 bays between the pillars with oak fences on layer 2, as a low rail. That is 18 fences. The space above stays open, so you can see into the hall.",[]],
["Stock the storerooms","Number the bays: along the back wall from the left, 1 to 6, and along each side wall from the back, 1 to 4, as you face the house. Everything goes against the wall, 2 high (layers 2–3). Along the back wall, stack barrels in bays 2, 4 and 6 (18 barrels). Along the left wall, stack hay bales in side bays 2 and 4 (12 hay bales). On both side walls, stack barrels in side bay 3 (12 barrels). That is 30 barrels and 12 hay bales.",[]],
["Fill the bookshelves","Along the back wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in bays 1, 3 and 5 (18 shelves). Along the right wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 shelves). That is 30 bookshelves, on layers 2–3.",[]],
["Set out the tables","Along the middle row of pillars, set out two tables: 2 oak slabs side by side between the second and third pillars from the left, and 2 more between the fifth and sixth. Give each table 4 chairs: an oak stair on each side of each slab, with the high side facing away from the table. That is 4 slabs and 8 stairs, all on layer 2.",[]],
["Lay the courtyard deck","The upper floor is U-shaped: a back wing 5 deep and two side wings 5 wide, around a courtyard 15 blocks wide and 12 deep that opens to the front. Start with the courtyard: lay oak planks on top of the pillars, on layer 5, over the whole courtyard, but leave out two things: a strip 3 wide in the middle, which is the path, and a hole 2 wide and 3 deep on each side of the path in the back half, one block away from the path and starting in the second row from the back wing. The two holes will be sunken crop beds. That is 132 planks.",[]],
["Floor the wings","Now lay spruce planks on layer 5 for the three wings: the back wing, 25 blocks by 5, and the two side wings, 5 blocks by 12 each. They join up in a U round the courtyard. That is 245 planks. This layer is also the ceiling of the ground-floor hall.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","Down the middle of the courtyard, fill the 3-wide strip you left with stone bricks, from the front edge to the back wing, on layer 5. That is 36 bricks. It lines up with the gap in the border.",[]],
["Hang the ground-floor lanterns","Under the ceiling, hang a lantern in the middle of every bay of the ground floor: 6 across and 4 deep, on layer 4, each one hanging from the layer above. That is 24 lanterns.",[]],
["Build the grand stairs: the base","In the gap in the border, build a solid block of stone bricks, 3 wide, that rises towards the house: 3 tall right against the front edge, 2 tall one block out, and 1 tall two blocks out. That is 18 bricks.",[]],
["Build the grand stairs: the steps","Along the top of that block, put stone brick stairs, rising towards the house with the high side facing it: one row of 3 on top of each column of bricks, plus one more row of 3 on the ground at the front. That makes 4 rows, 4 steps, up to the deck. That is 12 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the upper pillars","On the wing floors, stand pillars of oak logs, 3 tall (layers 6–8), in the same grid as before, but only on the wings. Leave out the middle pillar of the courtyard side of the back wing, where the door will be. That is 25 pillars, 75 logs.",[]],
["Wall the upper hall","Along the outer edge of the wings, which is the back, both outer sides and the two front ends, fill every bay between the pillars with oak planks on layer 6. That is 48 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the upper windows","Above each of those planks, put 2 glass panes (layers 7–8). That is 96 panes.",[]],
["Rail the courtyard side","On the courtyard side of the wings, fill the bays between the pillars with oak fences on layer 6, so the hall is open to the garden. Leave the 3-wide door open in the middle of the back wing. That is 28 fences.",[]],
["Fill the upper bookshelves","Along the back wall of the upper hall, stack bookshelves 2 high (layers 6–7) in bays 1, 2, 5 and 6 (24 shelves). Along the right wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 shelves). That is 36 bookshelves.",[]],
["Stock the upper hall","Between the bookshelves at the back, in bays 3 and 4, set a row of barrels, 1 high (6 barrels). On the left wall, stack hay bales 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 hay bales), and put one row of barrels in side bay 3 (3 barrels). Put a row of 3 barrels in side bay 3 of the right wall as well. That is 12 barrels and 12 hay bales.",[]],
["Set out the upper tables","In the middle of each side wing, set a table of 2 oak slabs side by side, in line with the middle row of pillars, with 4 chairs: an oak stair on each side of each slab, high side facing away from the table. That is 4 slabs and 8 stairs, on layer 6.",[]],
["Raise the roof posts","Now the roof farm. On top of each upper pillar, stack 2 more oak logs (layers 9–10), so the pillars stand up above the roof as posts. That is 25 posts, 50 logs.",[]],
["Frame the roof farm","The roof is a U-shaped field 5 blocks wide. Frame it with oak planks on layer 9: a border one block wide all the way round the U, on the outside, on the courtyard side, and across the two front ends, in the gaps between the posts. That is 79 planks. The middle of the field, 3 wide, stays empty for now.",[]],
["Fence the roof","On top of every border plank, put an oak fence on layer 10. On the courtyard side of the right wing, leave the bay between the two middle posts open, 3 blocks wide, because the roof stairs will arrive there. That is 76 fences.",[]],
["Plough the roof farm","Inside the border, fill the whole U, 3 blocks wide, with farmland on layer 9, except for a one-block channel down the middle of each strip: along the back wing, and down the middle of each side wing. Farmland also makes the ceiling of the upper hall. That is 92 farmland blocks.",[]],
["Flood the irrigation channel","Fill the channel with water on layer 9. It runs along the back wing from end to end, then turns and runs down the middle of each side wing to the front. One long channel waters every block of farmland on the roof. That is 49 water blocks.",[]],
["Hang the upper lanterns","Under the roof, hang a lantern from the middle of each bay of the upper hall (layer 8): 6 along the back wing and 3 in each side wing. That is 12 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the roof wheat","Plant wheat on every farmland block on the roof, on layer 10. That is 92 wheat.",[]],
["Light the roof","On layer 11, put a lantern on top of each of the 25 posts, and one more on the middle fence of each bay between two posts. That is 50 lanterns, and they give the roof its glow.",[]],
["Build the roof stairs","At the right-hand end of the courtyard, against the right wing, build a staircase 2 blocks wide and 4 rows long, rising towards the back, on top of the courtyard deck. Row 1: 2 oak stairs on layer 6. Row 2: 1 plank under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 7). Row 3: 2 planks under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 8). Row 4: 3 planks under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 9). Every stair has its high side facing the back. The top row is level with the roof, next to the gap in the fence. That is 12 planks and 8 stairs.",[]],
["Dig the two ponds","In each back corner of the courtyard, in the 2-wide strip beside the beds, build a small pond. Close each end with 2 stone bricks (layer 6), then fill the 2-by-4 space between them with water (layer 6). That is 8 stone bricks and 16 water.",[]],
["Build the crop beds","The garden has 4 beds, each a rectangle of stone bricks 4 wide and 5 deep, with a 2-by-3 space inside. Two sit at the back, against the back wing, round the holes you left in the deck, and two at the front, with one empty row between the back beds and the front beds. They stand on either side of the path, touching it. The 2 back beds are terraced higher: 2 bricks high (layers 6–7). The 2 front beds are 1 brick high (layer 6). That is 14 bricks round each bed, 84 in all.",[]],
["Fill the crop beds","Each bed has a 2-by-3 space inside. In each one, put a water block in the middle row of the left-hand column, and fill the other 5 blocks with farmland. In the two back beds this is the floor of the pit, on layer 5; in the two front beds it is on layer 6. That is 4 water and 20 farmland.",[]],
["Plant wheat and carrots","Plant wheat on all 5 farmland blocks of the back-left bed (layer 6), and on the middle block of the right-hand column of the front-left bed (layer 7). Plant carrots on the 4 farmland blocks of the back-right bed that are not in the middle of the right-hand column (layer 6), and on the middle block of the right-hand column of the front-right bed (layer 7). That is 6 wheat and 5 carrots.",[]],
["Plant pumpkins and melons","Put a pumpkin on each of the 4 corner farmland blocks of the front-left bed, and a melon on each of the 4 corner blocks of the front-right bed (layer 7). That is 4 pumpkins and 4 melons.",[]],
["Stand up the scarecrow","In the back-right bed, on the farmland block you left bare, build a scarecrow: 2 oak fences stacked on top of each other (layers 6–7), a hay bale on top of them (layer 8), an oak fence on each side of the bale along the bed as arms (layer 8), and a pumpkin on top as the head (layer 9). That is 4 fences (2 for the post, 2 for the arms), 1 hay bale and 1 pumpkin.",[]],
["Hedge the crop beds","On top of the stone rims, put leaves to make a low hedge (layer 8 on the two raised back beds, layer 7 on the front ones), leaving gaps where you like: about three of every four rim blocks. Mix them: mostly oak leaves with a flowering azalea leaf now and then. That is 31 oak leaves and 10 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Hedge the garden’s front edge","Along the front edge of the courtyard, on layer 6, plant a hedge of leaves on every deck block, except the 3 blocks of the path. That is 10 oak leaves and 2 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Make the farm corner","In the 2-wide strip at the front left of the courtyard, build a corner for the farm: a block of hay bales 2 wide, 2 deep on layer 6 with a second layer of 2 on the back row, then a gap of one block, then 2 rows of barrels, 2 wide (layer 6), with a flower pot on top of each barrel in the front row (layer 7). Then put 2 barrels and 2 flower pots in front of the roof stairs, at the front of the right-hand strip. That is 6 hay bales, 6 barrels and 4 flower pots.",[]],
["Plant the border hedge","Back down at the ground, plant a hedge along the stone border: leaves on layer 2 on top of about four in every five border slabs, with a flowering azalea leaf now and then. Keep the gap at the front clear. That is 54 oak leaves and 14 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Plant the two trees","Finish with two oak trees outside the border: one on the left, 2 blocks out from the border, level with the front of the house, and one on the right, 2 blocks out, near the back. Each has a trunk of 5 oak logs and a ball of leaves around the top, about 5 blocks across. That is 10 logs and 112 leaves for the pair.",[]]]},
{id:"sakura-teahouse",name:"Sakura Tea House",emoji:"🍵",tags:["Japanese","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Hard",time:"4 hr",size:"21×20",photos:[P11],pr:[1.329],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",photos:[P11],pr:[1.329],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A Japanese tea house with a cherry-wood veranda and frame, a sweeping dark roof, a torii gate over a stone path, a koi pond with a little bridge, and two blossoming cherry trees.",steps:[["Lay the stone plinth","On flat ground, mark out a rectangle 13 blocks wide and 11 deep, and fill it with stone bricks. This is the raised base of the tea house, 143 blocks. One of the long sides is the front, facing the path you will build later.",[]],
["Trim the plinth with stone slabs","Just outside the plinth, run a ring of stone brick slabs one block wide, all the way round, bottom half. This is a stone footing, and it makes the plinth look like it sits in the ground. The ring is 15 blocks by 13. In the middle of the front edge, leave a gap of 5 blocks: 3 for the front steps and one either side for the lantern posts, which come later. That is 47 slabs.",[]],
["Add the stone front steps","In the middle of the front edge, in the gap of the footing, set 3 stone brick stairs side by side with their high side against the plinth. This is the bottom step up to the house. Leave the blocks either side of them bare for now.",[]],
["Lay the cherry floor","Cover the plinth with cherry planks, one layer up, but leave a 3-wide notch in the middle of the front edge for the next step. That is 140 planks. This floor is also the veranda that runs round the house.",[]],
["Add the cherry front steps","Fill the notch with 3 cherry stairs, high side towards the house. With the stone steps below them they make a two-step staircase up to the veranda.",[]],
["Stand the four corner posts","The house itself is 9 blocks wide and 7 deep, set 2 blocks in from every edge of the plinth. On its four corners, stand posts of cherry logs, 5 blocks tall. That is 20 logs.",[]],
["Stand the wall posts","Along each wall, add more posts 5 tall, one every second block: 3 along the back wall, 2 on the front wall, and 2 on each side wall. Leave the 3 blocks in the middle of the front wall free for the doorway. That is 9 posts, 45 logs.",[]],
["Lay the skirt boards","On the bottom layer of the walls, fill each gap between posts with a cherry plank. The doorway stays open. That is 12 planks.",[]],
["Fill in the paper walls","Above the skirt boards, fill 6 of the 12 gaps with white concrete, like paper screens. These are the solid gaps: the outer gaps of the back wall and the two end gaps of each side wall. In each one stack white concrete (layer 4), a cherry plank as a crossbar (layer 5) and white concrete again (layer 6). The other 6 gaps are the windows, and wait for the next two steps. That is 12 white concrete and 6 cherry planks.",[]],
["Lay the window sills","In each of the 6 window gaps, lay a cherry plank on top of the skirt board as a sill (layer 4). These are the 2 middle gaps of the back wall, the 2 end gaps of the front wall and the middle gap of each side wall. The flower boxes will sit just in front of them. That is 6 planks.",[]],
["Set the window panes","Above each of the 6 sills, fill the window gap with glass panes, 2 tall: the 2 middle gaps of the back wall, the 2 end gaps of the front wall, and the middle gap of each side wall. That is 12 panes.",[]],
["Run the beam round the top","On the top layer, fill the gaps between the posts with cherry planks, including over the doorway so it makes a lintel. That is 15 planks. The walls are now 5 tall, and the doorway is 4 tall.",[]],
["Lay the tatami mats","Inside the house, lay moss carpet on the floor to look like tatami mats. The room is 7 blocks wide and 5 deep. Leave these spaces bare, because the next step fills them: the 3 blocks in the middle of the back row, a patch 3 wide and 2 deep in the middle of the room, 2 spaces on each side of that patch, and one space in each back corner. That is 20 carpets.",[]],
["Furnish the room","Stand 3 bookshelves side by side in the middle of the back row, 2 high. In the bare patch in the middle of the room, lay a tea table of 6 cherry slabs. Put a red carpet cushion in each of the 4 spaces either side of the table, and a lantern on the floor in each back corner. That is 6 bookshelves, 6 slabs, 4 red carpets and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Stand the veranda posts","Around the edge of the plinth, stand more cherry log posts, 5 tall: one on each corner, one in the middle of the back edge, two on each side edge, and two on the front edge, either side of the 3-block gap for the steps. That is 11 posts, 55 logs.",[]],
["Rail the veranda","On the layer above the floor, fill the gaps between the veranda posts with cherry fences, all the way round the plinth edge. Leave the gap in front of the steps open. That is 30 fences.",[]],
["Stand the lantern posts","On either side of the front steps, in the two spots left bare in the footing, stand a post of 3 cherry fences (layers 1–3) and set a lantern on top of each (layer 4). They frame the stairs, like lamp posts at the corners of a railing. That is 6 fences and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Add the roof brackets","Before the roof goes on, tuck cherry stairs against the veranda posts on the top layer (layer 7), with their high side against the post. They are the brackets that will hold up the eaves. Along the front, put 6: one on the inside of each corner post, and one either side of the two middle posts. Along the back, put 4: one on the inside of each corner post, and one either side of the middle post. On each side, put 2: one beside each of the two middle posts, facing the middle of the wall. That is 14 stairs. The lanterns will hang in the gaps between the brackets later.",[]],
["Set the flower boxes","On the veranda floor, in front of the windows, set a row of cherry trapdoors against the wall (layer 3) to be the flower boxes. Under each front window put 3, centred on the window: 6 in all. Under the two back windows put one long row of 5, from one block left of the left window to one block right of the right window. Under each side window put 3, along the wall. Leave the 3 blocks in front of the door free. That is 17 trapdoors.",[]],
["Plant the flowers","On top of each trapdoor, plant a flower (layer 4): pink tulips along the front and back, and alliums along the two sides. That is 11 pink tulips and 6 alliums.",[]],
["Roof: the pink eaves","The roof is built from rings, each ring one block further in than the last. The rings come in pairs, and each pair sits one layer higher than the one before. Start with the pink edge: a ring of cherry slabs, bottom half, sticking out one block past the plinth: 15 blocks by 13. At each of the four corners, lift the tip: use a full cherry plank instead of a slab on the corner block and on the block either side of it (12 planks), then put one slab on top of each corner plank. Just inside the ring, on the same layer, put a ring of cherry stairs resting on top of the veranda posts and brackets, high side facing in: 13 by 11, which is 44 stairs. Use the corner stair shape on the four corners. That is 12 planks, 44 slabs and 44 stairs, all cherry.",[]],
["Roof: the second layer","From here on the roof is dark oak, which makes the pink edge stand out. Inside the stairs ring, lay a ring of dark oak planks 11 by 9 (36 planks) and put a ring of dark oak slabs on top of it (36 slabs). Then lay a ring of planks 9 by 7 inside that (28 planks), which sits right on top of the house walls, and put a ring of dark oak stairs on top of it, high side facing in (28 stairs). That is 64 planks, 36 slabs and 28 stairs.",[]],
["Roof: the third layer","Same again, one layer higher. Lay a ring of planks 7 by 5 (20 planks) and put slabs on top of it (20 slabs). Then a ring of planks 5 by 3 inside that (12 planks), with stairs on top (12 stairs). That is 32 planks, 20 slabs and 12 stairs.",[]],
["Cap the ridge","The last ring leaves a gap 3 blocks long and 1 wide at the top. Fill it with 3 dark oak planks, level with the last stairs. On top of them, lay a dark oak plank in the middle and a slab at each end. Finish with a lightning rod on the middle plank as a finial. That is 4 planks, 2 slabs and 1 lightning rod.",[]],
["Lay the stone path","From the front steps, lay a path of stone brick slabs, 3 wide and 5 long, running straight out from the stone stairs. Then, from the last row of the path, lay a line of 2 stepping stones to the right, towards where the pond will go. That is 15 slabs for the path and 2 for the stones, 17 in all, on the bottom layer.",[]],
["Stand the torii pillars","The torii is the gate over the path. On the fourth row of the path, stand a cherry log pillar 5 tall on each side of it, leaving the 3 path blocks between them. That is 10 logs.",[]],
["Span the torii","Join the pillars with a row of 3 cherry planks between them on the fourth layer, then lay a row of 7 across the top of the pillars on the sixth layer, sticking out one block on each side. Join the two beams with one more plank in the middle, on the fifth layer. That is 11 planks.",[]],
["Cap the torii","On top of the long beam, lay a row of 9 cherry slabs, which sticks out one more block on each side and gives the gate its curved top. That is 9 slabs.",[]],
["Build the stone lanterns","On the first row of the path, one block either side of it, stack 2 stone bricks and put a lantern on top. That is 4 stone bricks and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Raise the pond rim","In the front right corner of the garden, as you face the house, leave a gap of 2 blocks from the path (the stepping stones will fill it) and mark a rectangle 7 wide and 5 deep. Trace its edge in stone bricks, one layer high. That is 20 blocks, with a 5 by 3 space in the middle.",[]],
["Fill the pond","Fill the 5 by 3 space inside the rim with water, except for the two corners at the right-hand end, where you put mossy cobblestone as rocks. That is 13 water blocks and 2 mossy cobblestone.",[]],
["Edge the pond with moss","Lay moss blocks on the grass just outside the rim, to soften the stone edge: 4 down the left side, leaving the row with the stepping stones clear, and 7 along the front. That is 11 moss blocks.",[]],
["Lay the bridge deck","The bridge is a 5-block arch across the pond, 3 wide, against the left side of the pond. First lay a deck of cherry planks over the water, 3 wide and 3 long, one layer up. Then put a second row of planks on top of the middle row of the deck, which makes the top of the arch. That is 9 + 3 = 12 planks.",[]],
["Add the arch stairs","Put cherry stairs on each end of the arch, always with the high side towards the middle of the bridge: a row of 3 on the rim at each end, and a row of 3 on the deck at each end, on top of the outer rows of planks. That is 12 stairs, 6 on the rim and 6 on the deck.",[]],
["Rail the bridge","Along both sides of the bridge, follow the arch with cherry fences, 5 on each side: the 3 over the middle on layer 4, and the 2 at the ends on layer 3. That is 10 fences.",[]],
["Light the bridge and the pond","Set a lantern on top of each of the 4 fences at the ends of the bridge rails (layer 4), and one on each of the 4 corners of the pond rim (layer 2). They light the water at night. That is 8 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the big trunk","In the front left corner, level with the torii, stand a 2 by 2 trunk of cherry logs, 7 tall. That is 28 logs.",[]],
["Add the branches","Four branches, each 3 logs. Start them on the sixth layer, touching each side of the trunk, run two blocks out, and turn the last log up by one block. That is 12 logs.",[]],
["Grow the lower canopy","Cover the trunk and branches with cherry leaves in a wide round shape on layers 7 and 8, then let a few hang down one layer lower, on layer 6, for a ragged edge. Leave a few gaps so it looks natural. That is 52 leaves: 3 on layer 6, 24 on layer 7 and 25 on layer 8.",[]],
["Grow the upper canopy","Build the top of the canopy on layers 9, 10 and 11, smaller than the layers below. That is 74 leaves.",[]],
["Plant the small trees","In the two back corners of the garden, plant a smaller tree each: a trunk of 4 cherry logs and a ball of cherry leaves around the top, about 3 blocks across. That is 8 logs and 53 leaves for the pair.",[]],
["Scatter the petals","On the grass under all three trees, scatter pink petals, about one block in three. Leave the stone footing bare. That is 33 pink petals.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Finally, hang lanterns from the underside of the roof, on the seventh layer: 3 across the front (one above the steps and one either side), 2 along the back either side of the middle post, and 1 in the middle of each side. Then hang one more from the middle of the torii's lower beam, on layer 3. That is 8 lanterns.",[]]]},
{id:"aldercrest-house",name:"Aldercrest House",emoji:"🏘️",tags:["Houses","Medieval","Showpiece"],diff:"Hard",time:"",size:"",photos:[P1,P2],pr:[1.018,1.049],desc:"A timber-framed house with a stepped stone base, a deep red roof and flower boxes under every window.",note:'Build reference: <a class="go" href="https://builditapp.com/aldercrest-house/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aldercrest House on Build It App</a>. These steps are written from the screenshots, so exact block counts aren’t listed yet.',steps:[["Lay the stone floor","Start with a stone brick floor, one block thick, for the main house and the smaller wing joined to its side. Together the two rectangles are the footprint of the whole house.",[["Stone Bricks"]]],
["Add the porch slab and steps","In front of the main house, lay a small stone slab for the porch. Then add the stepped stone brick stairs: a row along the front, and another down the left side.",[["Stone Bricks"],["Stone Brick Stairs"]]],
["Raise the stone base wall","Build a one-block wall of stone bricks round the edge of both floors. This is the low base the timber house will sit on.",[["Stone Bricks"]]],
["Stand the main house posts","On the base, stand tall oak log posts, 4 blocks high, at the corners and at every other block along the walls of the main house.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Stand the wing posts","Do the same for the wing: posts at the corners and every other block along its walls.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Run the ground-floor beams","Join the tops of the posts with a row of oak logs along every wall. This is the horizontal timber that makes the frame.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Plaster the ground floor","Fill the gaps between the posts and under the beams with plain oak planks, for the plastered look. Leave openings where the windows go, and a doorway at the front of the main house.",[["Oak Planks"]]],
["Set the ground-floor windows","Fill the window openings with glass panes. Flank each window with an open trapdoor as a shutter if you like.",[["Glass Pane"],["Oak Trapdoor"]]],
["Lay the upper floor","Lay a floor of oak planks over the whole ground floor, pushing it out one block past the front and left walls so the upper storey overhangs.",[["Oak Planks"]]],
["Add the oak brackets","Hold up the overhang with oak log brackets along the front and left edges, one under each upper post.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Raise the upper posts","On top of the overhang, stand oak log posts lined up with the ones below: 3 blocks high on the main house, 2 on the wing.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Plaster the upper floor","Fill between the upper posts with oak planks, leaving window openings in the same pattern as downstairs.",[["Oak Planks"]]],
["Set the upper windows","Fill the upper openings with glass panes.",[["Glass Pane"]]],
["Frame the main gables","Build the steep, stepped gable ends of the main house first, in oak planks, getting narrower as they rise: 8 wide, then 6, 4 and 2.",[["Oak Planks"]]],
["Roof the main house, lower slopes","Cover the two slopes with deep red nether brick stairs, starting at the eaves, then the row above. The row along the front gable uses oak stairs.",[["Red Nether Brick Stairs"],["Oak Stairs"]]],
["Roof the main house, top and ridge","Add the last two rows of stairs on each slope, then close the ridge with red nether bricks.",[["Red Nether Brick Stairs"],["Red Nether Bricks"],["Oak Stairs"]]],
["Frame the wing gables","Build the smaller gable ends of the side wing in oak planks, 4 wide and then 2.",[["Oak Planks"]]],
["Roof the wing","Cover the wing with three rows of red stairs on each side, finishing the front end with oak stairs.",[["Red Nether Brick Stairs"],["Oak Stairs"]]],
["Hang the flower boxes","Hang trapdoor boxes under the windows, upstairs and down, along the front of the house and the wing. Fill each one with a rose bush.",[["Oak Trapdoor"],["Rose Bush"]]],
["Stand the porch posts","On the porch slab, stand two oak log posts 3 blocks high, a few blocks apart, to carry the awning.",[["Oak Log"]]],
["Roof the porch awning","Cover the porch with a small red awning: stairs on the outside edge, stairs sloping back toward the house wall, and red nether bricks underneath.",[["Red Nether Brick Stairs"],["Red Nether Bricks"]]],
["Lay the brick path","Lay a short path of bricks, two blocks wide, leading out from the porch.",[["Bricks"]]],
["Hang the lanterns","Finish with lanterns: two under the porch awning, four along the front eaves of the overhang, and two under the overhang on the left side.",[["Lantern"]]]]},
{id:"brindlewood-manor",name:"Brindlewood Manor",emoji:"🏡",tags:["Houses","Medieval","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"7 hr",size:"23×20",photos:[P10],pr:[1.273],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A walled-garden manor in the medieval timber-and-stone style: a stone ground floor, a plastered and timber-framed upper floor, front balconies, dormers, cobblestone-trimmed gables, a porch, a well, trees and a vegetable bed.",steps:[["Mark out the garden and lay the wall base","On flat ground, mark a 23×20 rectangle for the garden. The side with the gate is the front. Lay one course of stone bricks around the whole outline, but leave a 3-wide gap in the middle of the front side for the gate. That is 79 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the cobblestone wall and pillars","Go round again with a course of cobblestone on top of the stone bricks, and swap in about 4 mossy cobblestone blocks, scattered, so it looks weathered. At 15 spots, build a 4-high stone brick pillar instead: the four corners, both sides of the gate gap, and the rest every 5 or 6 blocks along the walls. The model shows exactly where.",[]],
["Rail the wall, cap the pillars and span the gate","Put an oak fence on top of every cobblestone block that is not a pillar. Cap the 9 pillars that are not at a corner or beside the gate with a stone brick slab. Leave the six corner and gate pillars bare, because they get lanterns later. Finish the gate with a 3-block dark oak plank beam across the gap, level with the pillar tops.",[]],
["Lay the stone plinth","Inside the garden, mark the house: a 15×7 main block with a 7-wide wing sticking out 4 blocks from the front, so the whole house is 15×11. Leave 3 blocks of space between the house and the garden wall at the back and on both sides, and 4 blocks at the front. Lay a one-block ring of stone bricks along that outline, mixing in about 7 mossy stone bricks. Only the outline is needed for now; lay any floor you like inside later.",[]],
["Add the door steps","Place three oak stairs in front of the wing, centered on the front door spot, with their tall backs toward the house. Then place one more oak stair at the middle of the back wall, for the back door.",[]],
["Set the dark oak posts","On top of the plinth, stand dark oak logs 3 blocks high at 12 spots: every outside corner, the two inside corners where the wing meets the main block, one either side of the front door spot, and two on the back wall, 3 blocks either side of the middle.",[]],
["Fill in the main block's walls","Fill between the posts of the main block with cobblestone, 3 blocks high: the back wall, both end walls and the two short stretches of front wall either side of the wing. Mix in about 14 mossy cobblestone. Leave a 1×2 doorway in the middle of the back wall and put a dark oak plank across the top of it. At the 6 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: two on the front, two on the back and one in the middle of each end wall.",[]],
["Fill in the front wing's walls","Do the same for the wing: its front wall and its two side walls, 3 blocks high in cobblestone, with about 4 mossy blocks mixed in. Leave a 1×2 doorway in the middle of the front wall and put a dark oak plank across the top. At the 4 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: one on each side of the front door, just past the posts, and one in the middle of each side wall.",[]],
["Glaze the ground-floor windows","Fill the two open blocks above each of those 10 window spots, 6 on the main block and 4 on the wing, with glass panes. That makes ten 2-tall windows, 20 panes altogether.",[]],
["Lay the floor beam","Run a one-block ring of dark oak planks around the top of the walls, following the whole outline. It carries the upper floor and shows as the dark band between the two storeys.",[]],
["Build the balcony decks","At each end of the front, add a 4×2 oak plank deck on the outer side of the wing, level with the floor beam and touching the main front wall. Hold each deck up with two dark oak logs, 4 blocks tall, under its two outer corners (the ones away from the house).",[]],
["Fence the balconies","Put oak fence along the long outer edge of each deck and along its outer end. The back edge sits against the house and the inner end against the wing wall, so leave those two sides open. That is 5 fence blocks per balcony.",[]],
["Frame the upper floor","Continue the dark oak posts up another 3 blocks from the floor beam at the same corners as downstairs, but leave out the two posts beside the front door, because that wall becomes a wide window. That is 10 posts.",[]],
["Plaster the main block's upper walls","Fill between the posts of the main block with smooth sandstone, 3 blocks high. On the front wall, make a 1×2 doorway onto each balcony, directly above the ground-floor windows, and put a dark oak plank across the top of each one. At the 7 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: one beside each balcony doorway toward the end of the house, three on the back wall (the middle and one either side) and one in the middle of each end wall.",[]],
["Plaster the wing's upper walls","Do the same for the wing: smooth sandstone, 3 blocks high, along its front wall and both side walls. At the 5 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: three side by side over the front door, and one in the middle of each side wall.",[]],
["Glaze the upper windows","Fill the two open blocks above each of the 12 window spots with glass panes. The three spots over the front door sit side by side, so that window reads as one wide window. That is 24 panes in all.",[]],
["Fill the main gable ends","On each end wall of the main block, build a triangle of smooth sandstone on top of the wall: 5 blocks wide in the bottom row, 3 in the middle row and 1 on top. Swap the middle block of the middle row for a glass pane. The roof will overhang this by one block.",[]],
["Main roof, row 1: the eaves","Start with the lowest row of the roof on one long side. Lay a line of spruce stairs sticking out one block past the wall, with the low step facing outward and the tall back toward the middle of the house. Behind it, lay a line of spruce planks directly on top of the wall. Run it the full 15 blocks between the two gable walls, then repeat on the other long side.",[]],
["Main roof, row 2","One block higher and one block closer to the middle: a line of spruce stairs sitting on top of row 1's planks, with a line of planks behind them. Run it the full 15 blocks again, on both sides.",[]],
["Main roof, row 3","The same again, one block higher and one block in: stairs on top of row 2's planks, planks behind them, on both sides.",[]],
["Main roof, row 4 and the ridge","The top row is two lines of stairs, one on each side, with a single line of spruce planks between them along the very top. That line is the ridge, and the two sides of the roof now meet in the middle.",[]],
["Trim the main gables in cobblestone","Along both gable ends, replace the outermost column of the roof with cobblestone: cobblestone stairs where the spruce stairs would go, plain cobblestone where the planks would go. This column sticks out one block past the gable wall and follows the same four-row stair-step shape.",[]],
["Frame the dormer windows","Mark five dormers: two on the front slope, one above each end of the house, and three on the back slope, one in the middle and one 5 blocks either side. Each dormer stands on row 2 of the roof: two dark oak logs with a glass pane between them.",[]],
["Roof the dormers","Give each dormer a small 3-wide gable. In front, put a cobblestone stair on each side with the tall back toward the middle and a cobblestone block between them. Directly behind that, up the slope, repeat the shape in spruce: spruce stairs on each side and a spruce plank in the middle.",[]],
["Fill the front gable end","On the wing's front wall, build the same sandstone triangle as the main gables: 5 blocks wide, then 3, then 1, with a glass pane in the middle of the middle row.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 1: the eaves","Roof the wing the same way as the main house, working from the front wall back toward the main roof. Row 1: along each side wall, a line of spruce stairs overhanging the wall by one block, tall backs toward the middle of the wing, with a line of spruce planks on top of the wall behind it. It is only 3 blocks long on each side, because the main roof already covers the rest.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 2","One block higher and one block in, on both sides: stairs on top of row 1's planks, planks behind them. This row is 4 blocks long on each side, and its far end touches the main roof's second row.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 3","Another block higher and in: stairs and planks again, now 5 blocks long on each side, running back until they meet the main roof.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 4 and the ridge","Two lines of stairs, 6 blocks long, with a line of spruce planks between them along the top for the wing's ridge. It runs from the main ridge out to the front and ends at the same height as the main one.",[]],
["Trim the front gable","Replace the outermost line of the wing roof at the front end with cobblestone stairs and cobblestone blocks, sticking out one block past the gable wall, to match the main gables.",[]],
["Build the chimney","Near the left end of the main ridge, as you face the front, stand a 2×2 chimney on the roof, straddling the ridge with half of it over the back slope. Build it 2 blocks high in stone bricks, then cap it with 4 dark oak logs.",[]],
["Add the front porch","Over the front door, make a 5×2 porch. Stand a dark oak log post, 4 blocks tall, at each outer corner of the front edge. Lay dark oak planks across the top, two rows of five, one row against the house wall and one over the posts. Roof it with a row of spruce planks against the wall and a row of spruce stairs on the outer edge, tall backs toward the house.",[]],
["Set the flower pots","Put a flower pot against the wall under each of the seven upper windows on the front, back and ends, plus one pot on each balcony deck, just in front of the balcony doorway. That is 9 pots.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang a lantern from the porch beam on each side of the path, from the underside of each of the four lowest corner stairs of the main roof, and from under each balcony deck, between the two support posts. That is 8 hanging lanterns.",[]],
["Stand the balcony lanterns","Stand a lantern on the fence post at each end of every balcony's outer rail. That is 4 lanterns, and they light the balcony doors.",[]],
["Build the well base","In the front-left of the garden, as you face the house, lay a 3×3 square of cobblestone with the middle block replaced by water.",[]],
["Roof the well and hang the chains","Stand an oak fence 2 blocks high at each corner of the well. On top, lay a ring of spruce stairs around the edge, tall backs toward the middle, a spruce plank in the center and a spruce slab on top of that. Hang two chains from the center plank down toward the water.",[]],
["Plant the tree trunks","Plant two trees: one in the front-right of the garden and one in the back-left. Each trunk is 4 oak logs tall.",[]],
["Grow the leaf crowns","Wrap each trunk in a rounded ball of oak leaves, about 5 blocks across. Work from the bottom: a plus shape around the trunk, then a 3×3 with the four tips added, then the widest layer, a 5×5 with the corners cut off, then another 3×3 with tips, then a plus on top. Swap about 5 leaves on each tree for flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Make the vegetable bed","In the back-right corner, mark a 5×3 bed and put one water block in the middle. Hoe the other 14 blocks into farmland and plant wheat on each one. The water in the middle keeps all of it moist. If you want it level with the grass, dig the bed down one block first.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","Lay a 3-wide path of cobblestone slabs from the gate, through the gap, to the front steps: 4 slabs long, so 12 in all.",[]],
["Add the lamp posts and gate lanterns","Beside the path, just beyond the porch, build two lamp posts: 2 oak fence blocks tall with a lantern standing on top. Put a lantern on the gate beam, and one on top of each of the six bare pillars: the four corners and the two beside the gate.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Plant 14 small bushes around the foot of the house and along the garden walls: nine single blocks of oak leaves and five that are 2 blocks tall, with about half of them topped with flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Add barrels and roses","Put a barrel on each side of the front steps, then plant four rose bushes: two beside the porch posts and one beside each end of the house.",[]]]},
{id:"cosy-woodland-lodge",name:"Cosy Woodland Lodge",emoji:"🍂",tags:["Houses","Cozy","Nature"],diff:"Medium",time:"",size:"",photos:[P3],pr:[1.045],photoCredit:"Image from @ireniepooh’s TikTok video of this build.",desc:"A rustic timber-frame lodge with a steep dark roof, a tall cream chimney and warm lantern light.",note:'Build by @ireniepooh on TikTok. These steps are written from a short video, so the block names are best guesses and no block counts are listed.',steps:[["Mark the footprint","Outline the floor plan from above. It isn’t a plain rectangle: it’s a main house with a smaller wing or porch attached. The video labels the edge lengths 9, 5, 6, 11, 5, 4, 5 and 5 blocks, so measure carefully before placing anything solid.",[]],
["Lay the main floor","Lay a pale stone brick floor over the main house part of the outline.",[["Stone Bricks"]]],
["Lay the wing and porch floors","Carry the stone floor across the smaller wing, and add a small stone porch in front of the main house.",[["Stone Bricks"]]],
["Set the main house posts","Stand dark oak log posts, 5 blocks high, at every corner and at every other block along the outline of the main house.",[["Dark Oak Log"]]],
["Set the wing posts","Do the same for the wing, with posts 3 blocks high.",[["Dark Oak Log"]]],
["Fill the main walls","Fill between the posts with orange-brown terracotta so the dark logs read as timber framing. Leave a doorway in the front wall, and gaps where the windows will go.",[["Terracotta"]]],
["Fill the wing walls","Fill the wing walls the same way, 3 blocks high, leaving gaps for windows.",[["Terracotta"]]],
["Build the gable walls","Above the main walls, build the triangular gable ends in terracotta at the front and back of the main house, narrowing as they rise. Close in the end of the wing the same way.",[["Terracotta"]]],
["Glaze the windows","Set glass into the window openings, including the small windows in the front gable.",[["Glass Pane"]]],
["Outline the main roof","Frame the steep roof of the main house first. Along each gable edge, place a row of grey stone brick stairs stepping up to the ridge, so you can see the shape before you fill it in.",[["Stone Brick Stairs"]]],
["Outline the wing roof","Do the same on the end of the wing.",[["Stone Brick Stairs"]]],
["Roof the main house, eaves","Starting at the eaves, run the first row of dark oak stairs along each slope.",[["Dark Oak Stairs"]]],
["Roof the main house, middle","Add the next row of dark stairs up each slope.",[["Dark Oak Stairs"]]],
["Roof the main house, top and ridge","Add the top row of stairs on each slope, then finish the ridge with dark oak planks.",[["Dark Oak Stairs"],["Dark Oak Planks"]]],
["Roof the wing","Cover the wing’s slopes with two rows of dark oak stairs on each side, and run a dark plank ridge along the top.",[["Dark Oak Stairs"],["Dark Oak Planks"]]],
["Set the porch posts","On the porch, stand four dark oak posts 3 blocks high, one at each corner.",[["Dark Oak Log"]]],
["Roof the porch","Give the front porch its own small roof in oak stairs, meeting in the middle.",[["Oak Stairs"]]],
["Raise the chimney, lower half","Build a tall, narrow chimney in a pale cream block, 2 blocks wide, rising out of the ridge. Start with the first 3 blocks.",[["Smooth Sandstone"]]],
["Raise the chimney, upper half","Carry it on up until it stands well above the roofline. Smoke from the top is a nice finishing touch.",[["Smooth Sandstone"]]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang two lanterns under the porch roof, and stand two more on the ground beside the wing.",[["Lantern"]]],
["Raise the lamp post","By the path, build a lamp post of dark oak logs, 4 blocks tall, topped with a lantern.",[["Dark Oak Log"],["Lantern"]]],
["Dress the garden","Finish with barrels beside the wing and small bushes of leaves around the base of the house.",[["Barrel"],["Oak Leaves"]]]]},
{id:"cozy-cottage",name:"Cozy Cottage",emoji:"🏡",tags:["Houses","Survival","Cozy"],diff:"Medium",time:"2 hr",size:"14×13",photos:[P5],pr:[1.651],photoCredit:"",desc:"A snug stone-footed cottage with barred cellar windows, a covered porch, white plaster walls between log pillars, a cross-gabled spruce roof, a brick chimney and lanterns by the door.",steps:[["Build the stone foundation","Mark out a rectangle 12 blocks wide and 9 deep on flat ground; the long side with the porch and door is the front. Trace its edge with a ring of stone, one block thick and 3 tall (layers 1–3). Mix the blocks as you go: mostly stone bricks, with cobblestone, mossy cobblestone and mossy stone bricks scattered through it, so the base looks old. In the front wall, leave two gaps on the middle layer (layer 2), each 2 wide: the 3rd and 4th blocks from the left, and the 6th and 7th. They are the cellar windows. That is 110 blocks: 49 stone bricks, 29 cobblestone, 21 mossy cobblestone and 11 mossy stone bricks.",[]],
["Bar the cellar windows","Fill each window gap with oak fences to make bars: 4 fences. Then, inside the cellar, one block back from each window pair, light it up: stand a stone brick on the ground (layer 1) behind the 4th block from the left and behind the 7th, and put a lantern on each (layer 2). That is 2 bricks and 2 lanterns, so the windows glow from outside.",[]],
["Lay the main floor","On top of the foundation, on layer 4, lay planks over the whole 12 × 9 rectangle. Use oak planks for the front-left corner, the porch, which is 7 wide and 2 deep, and spruce planks for the rest. That is 14 oak planks and 94 spruce planks.",[]],
["Build the base of the entrance steps","In front of the 8th to 11th blocks from the left of the front wall, build a stepped pile of stone bricks, 3 rows deep. The outer two columns (the 8th and the 11th from the left) are 1 tall in the front row (layer 1), 2 tall in the middle row, and 3 tall in the row against the house. The two inner columns (the 9th and 10th) are empty in the front row, 1 tall in the middle row, and 2 tall against the house. Then put a flower pot on top of the right-hand column in the middle row (layer 3) and an allium on top of the left-hand one. That is 18 bricks, 1 pot and 1 allium.",[]],
["Lay the entrance stairs","Between the two stone columns, put oak stairs 2 wide, with the high side facing the house: the front row on the ground (layer 1), the middle row on top of the 1-tall bricks (layer 2), and the last row on top of the 2-tall bricks, against the foundation (layer 3). They climb to the floor. That is 6 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the log pillars","Stand 14 pillars of oak log on the floor, each 3 tall (layers 5–7). Count rows from the back (row 1) to the front (row 9), and blocks from the left (block 1) to the right (block 12). Back wall (row 1): blocks 1, 4, 8 and 12. Right wall (block 12): rows 5 and 9. Front of the wing: block 8, rows 8 and 9. Back wall of the porch (row 7): blocks 1, 4 and 7. Left wall (block 1): rows 4 and 9. Front of the porch (row 9): block 7. That is 42 logs.",[]],
["Wall in with white concrete","The house is a main block 12 wide and 7 deep (rows 1–7), with a wing at the front right, blocks 8–12 and rows 8–9. The front left, blocks 1–7 and rows 8–9, is the open porch. Between the pillars, fill the outside walls with white concrete on layers 5 and 6: the back wall, the left wall, the right wall, the front of the wing, the left side of the wing (block 8, rows 8–9), and the back wall of the porch (row 7). Leave two doorways open on both layers: block 10 of row 9 (the front door) and block 2 of row 7 (the porch door). On layer 6 only, also leave 8 window gaps and 3 niches: windows at blocks 3, 6 and 10 of the back wall; rows 3 and 7 of the right wall; rows 3 and 5 of the left wall; and block 6 of the porch back wall; niches at blocks 9 and 11 of row 9 and block 5 of row 7. That is 35 concrete blocks.",[]],
["Lay the roof beams","On layer 7, on top of every gap between the pillars in the outside walls, lay spruce planks, including over the doorways, windows and niches. That is 25 planks, one for every wall gap.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Put a glass pane in each of the 8 window gaps on layer 6: three in the back wall, two in each side wall, and one in the back wall of the porch. That is 8 panes.",[]],
["Hang the doors and lanterns","Stack 2 oak planks in each doorway for a door (layers 5–6): the front door at block 10 of row 9, and the porch door at block 2 of row 7. That is 4 planks. Then stand a lantern in each of the 3 niches (layer 6), so one glows either side of the front door and one by the porch window. That is 3 lanterns.",[]],
["Rail the porch","On layer 5, put oak fences along the front of the porch, blocks 2 to 6 of row 9, and one at the left end, block 1 of row 8. That is 6 fences. The corner pillars stand either side.",[]],
["Furnish the porch","On layer 5 along the back wall of the porch (row 8): a bench of three spruce slabs, bottom half, at blocks 2, 3 and 4; a pink tulip at block 5; a flower pot at block 6; and a barrel at block 7. That is 3 slabs, 1 tulip, 1 pot and 1 barrel.",[]],
["Fill in the gables","Above every wall column, including the pillars, fill with white concrete from layer 8 up to the layer just under where the roof will be. The roof is one block bigger than the house all round and rises one layer for each row you move in from the front or back edge, so counting rows from the one behind the back wall (row 0) the roof stair sits at layer 8, 9, 10, 11 and 12 for rows 0 to 4, and at layer 13 on the ridge, row 5. That makes the fill: the back wall 1 tall, the left wall 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3 tall for rows 1–7, and the right wall 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 tall for rows 1–9. The porch back wall (row 7) is 3 tall across, and the front of the wing is 1, 2, 3, 2, 1 tall for blocks 8–12, with a glass pane in the middle of its second row (layer 9) and a lantern under it on the beam (layer 8). That is 83 concrete, 1 pane and 1 lantern.",[]],
["Roof the eaves","The roof is one block bigger than the house on every side: 14 blocks long (blocks 0–13) and 11 rows deep (rows 0–10). Put a stair on every column at the height for its row, with the high side facing the ridge. In this step do the outer rim: dark oak stairs on layer 8 (rows 0 and 10) and spruce stairs on layer 9 (rows 1 and 9), with a spruce plank under each layer-9 stair on layer 8 wherever there is no concrete. Over the wing, blocks 7–13 of rows 7–10, a second roof runs front to back instead, rising one layer for each block in from block 7 or 13 up to a ridge down block 10; for each column use whichever of the two roofs is higher, and face its stairs along that slope. That is 23 dark oak stairs, 27 spruce stairs and 13 planks.",[]],
["Roof the middle","Continue up the slope: spruce stairs on layers 10 and 11 (rows 2 and 8, then rows 3 and 7), each with a spruce plank under it on the layer below, and the same on the wing roof. That is 59 stairs and 43 planks.",[]],
["Roof the top rows","Next, rows 4 and 6: dark oak stairs on layer 12, each with a spruce plank under it on layer 11, all along the roof. That is 28 stairs and 24 planks.",[]],
["Cap the ridge","Along the ridge, row 5, lay dark oak slabs, bottom half, on layer 13, with a spruce plank under each on layer 12. Then cap the ridge of the front gable the same way: slabs on layer 11 down block 10 of rows 8 to 10, with a plank under each on layer 10. That is 17 slabs and 14 planks.",[]],
["Build the chimney","On the back slope of the roof, on block 9 of row 4, stack 3 bricks (layers 13–15) and put a barrel on top (layer 16) for the chimney pot. That is 3 bricks and 1 barrel.",[]],
["Hang the porch lanterns","Hang a lantern from the roof in the middle of the porch, block 4 of row 8 (layer 8), and two more at the front edge of the porch under the roof, blocks 2 and 6 of row 9 (layer 7). That is 3 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the front garden","In the strip one block in front of the cellar wall (row 10): alliums at blocks 2 and 4, pink tulips at blocks 3 and 6, and a bush of 2 flowering azalea leaves, stacked, at block 7 beside the steps. That is 2 alliums, 2 tulips and 2 azalea leaves.",[]],
["Stack the barrels","Beside the front right corner, outside the 12th block, put two barrels on the ground at rows 8 and 9, and a third on top of the one at row 9. That is 3 barrels.",[]]]},
{id:"treehouse-hideaway",name:"Oak Treehouse Hideaway",emoji:"🌳",tags:["Nature","Cozy","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"5 hr",size:"28×24",photos:[P6],pr:[1.299],photoCredit:"",desc:"A big oak treehouse on a buttressed trunk: a plank cabin with a fenced balcony under a wide roof, a huge lumpy crown, a leafy shelf with a rope swing, a door under a slab awning, a stream with stepping stones, bamboo, flowers and little red mushrooms.",steps:[["Dig the stream","Find open ground about 28 blocks wide and 24 deep. The side with the door is the front; left and right are as you face the tree from the front. Mark where the trunk will stand, a 5 × 5 square, and note its centre block. The stream runs all the way across the front, 2 blocks wide and 27 long, with its near edge 7 to 9 blocks in front of the centre block, drifting a block or so forward and back as it goes. Dig it 1 block down so the water is level with the grass and fill it with water. Directly in front of the trunk, at the centre block and the one to its left, leave a 2 × 2 gap for the path to cross. That is 50 water blocks (layer 1).",[]],
["Lay the stepping stones","Smooth sandstone slabs, flat on the ground, make a path 2 wide from the door out over the stream. Count 8 rows going out from the trunk, the first one right in front of where the awning will end (it sticks out 2 blocks from the trunk). Use both blocks in each row except: in row 1 use only the left block, and leave row 7 empty so it looks like stepping stones. The left block of the path is on the line of the door, the second block from the left of the trunk's front face, and the right block is the one beside it. Rows 4 and 5 fill the gap in the stream; the water will flow in around the slabs. That is 13 slabs (layer 1).",[]],
["Grow the root buttresses","Round the trunk square build 26 columns of oak logs of different heights, so the foot of the tree flares out like roots. The grid shows each column's height in blocks. Rows run from the back to the front and columns from left to right, T marks the trunk square (built next) and a dot means no log. row 1: · · 2 3 2 3 2 · ·; row 2: · 3 T T T T T 2 ·; row 3: 2 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 4: 3 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 5: 2 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 6: · 3 T T T T T 2 ·; row 7: · · 1 1 1 1 1 · ·. The 5 tall columns on the left are the tallest steps; the single row along the front is the doorstep. That is 58 logs (layers 1–5).",[]],
["Raise the lower trunk","Inside the ring, build the trunk square, 5 × 5, 6 blocks tall out of oak logs (layers 1–6). On the front face, leave the second block from the left empty on layers 2 and 3: that is the door. It is 148 logs, 150 minus the 2 door spots. The logs show their bark on every side, so the trunk looks wide and solid.",[]],
["Plank up the right side","Against the right face of the trunk, on its middle 3 blocks, build a strip of oak planks 3 wide, 1 thick and 10 tall (layers 3–12). Make two windows in its middle column: replace the planks on layers 5–6 and layers 8–9 with glass panes. Under each window, on layers 5 and 8, put an oak slab, bottom half, in the block just outside the lower pane, as a windowsill. That is 26 planks, 4 panes and 2 slabs. The strip starts on top of the 2 tall ledge on that side.",[]],
["Hang the door and awning","In the 2 door spots on the front face, stack 2 spruce planks (layers 2–4). Put an oak fence post on each side of the door, in front of the trunk on the doorstep row, 2 tall each: that is 4 fences. Roof the entrance with oak slabs, bottom half, on layer 4: 5 across, from one block left of the left post to one block right of the right post, and 2 deep, so it sticks out 2 blocks in front of the trunk. That is 2 planks and 10 slabs.",[]],
["Narrow the trunk","Above the ledges, carry the trunk up another 6 layers (layers 7–12), still 5 × 5 but with the 4 corner logs left out so it narrows. That is 126 logs, 21 on each layer. The trunk now reaches layer 12.",[]],
["Build the platform","On layer 13, at the top of the trunk, lay a floor of oak planks 11 wide and 7 deep. It sits on the plank strip at the right and stretches out 5 blocks past the trunk on the left, where it becomes the balcony. Leave the middle 3 × 3 free: the trunk goes on through it. That is 68 planks.",[]],
["Carry the trunk through","In the 3 × 3 gap in the floor, stack oak logs 9 tall (layers 13–21), so the trunk climbs through the cabin and keeps going above the roof. That is 81 logs.",[]],
["Raise the cabin walls","On the platform, build the walls of the cabin as a ring of oak planks 7 wide and 7 deep, 4 tall (layers 14–17). Three of the walls sit flush with the edge of the floor, and the left wall faces the balcony. Leave out a doorway in the middle of the left wall, 2 tall, and a window opening 3 wide and 2 tall in the middle of the front wall and the middle of the right wall, on layers 15–16. That is 82 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Fill the two window openings with glass panes: 3 across and 2 up, 12 panes (layers 15–16). The panes join up into one big window on each wall.",[]],
["Fence the balcony","On layer 14, set oak fence along the left edge of the floor, 7 blocks, and along the front and back edges of the balcony, 3 blocks each: that is 13 fences. Then raise 3 posts from the left edge, at its two ends and in the middle, with 3 more fences on top of each (layers 15–17), to hold up the roof. That is 22 fences.",[]],
["Lay the roof","On layer 18, lay a flat roof of oak planks 12 wide and 9 deep, with the trunk coming up through the middle: it covers the cabin, sticks out 1 block at the front, back and right, and reaches over the balcony to the tops of the posts. That is 99 planks. Then hang 2 lanterns from the underside of the roof inside the cabin (layer 17), in the front left corner and the back right corner, 1 block in from the walls.",[]],
["Top the roof","On layer 19, over the cabin, lay a 7 × 7 square of oak slabs, bottom half, round the trunk. That is 40 slabs. It gives the roof its stepped edge.",[]],
["Grow the branches","Above the roof the trunk keeps climbing: stack 7 more logs in the middle, layers 22–28. On the right of the trunk, standing on the roof cap, build a stub of 4 logs: 2 stacked beside the trunk (layers 20–21), then 1 to their right on layer 21 and 1 on top of that on layer 22. About 5 blocks above the roof, put four arms of logs sticking out from the trunk on layer 24, 8 blocks to the left and right and 7 forward and back, so the leaves have something to hang from; they will mostly be hidden. Finally, build the branch for the lower leaf shelf: from the plank strip on the right, 2 logs side by side going out on layer 10, starting against the plank strip, then 1 on top of the second one on layer 11 and 4 more going out along layer 11 (that is 7 logs). That is 48 logs in all (layers 10–28).",[]],
["Start the crown: the underside","The crown is built as a lumpy ball of leaves, a shell 2 blocks thick, and this is the one step where you shape things by eye. It is made of one big lump, about 17 wide and 14 deep, centred over the trunk, with smaller lumps on the left, right, top and front, and one hanging low over the roof. Start with the underside, layers 21–24: the hanging lump over the roof and the bottom of the big lump and the left lump. Make the edge ragged by leaving out a leaf here and there. That is 255 leaves.",[]],
["The widest part","On layers 25–29, the crown is at its widest, 24 blocks across and 15 deep. Carry the shell of the big lump up round the arms, bulge the left lump out to the left, and add the right lump about 5 blocks past the trunk. Keep the shell 2 thick and leave a few small holes so the light gets in. That is 578 leaves.",[]],
["Close the top","On layers 30–35, bring the crown in towards the top: the big lump closes over, and the top lump sits on it a little to the left, about 10 blocks wide. Round the top off and leave the edge uneven. That is 441 leaves.",[]],
["Make the lower leaf shelf","On the right of the cabin, round the branch, build a shelf of leaves about 9 wide and 10 deep, 3 layers thick (layers 11–13). Layers 12 and 13 are almost solid; the bottom layer, layer 11, is ragged, with gaps, but make sure the 4 blocks above the swing beam, in the row one in front of the branch, are leaves. That is 145 leaves.",[]],
["Hang the swing ropes","Under the shelf, one block in front of the branch, place a beam of 4 oak fences in a row on layer 10. From the first and last fence hang a rope of 7 fences, down to layer 3. That is 18 fences (layers 3–10).",[]],
["Add the swing seat","Under the ropes, on layer 2, lay 4 oak slabs in a row, bottom half, for the seat. That is 4 slabs.",[]],
["Plant the bamboo","Plant bamboo in 18 loose clumps, 9 on each side of the tree and the stream, with a few behind and a few at the front, round the corners of the plot and along the stream. Each clump is a patch about 3 × 3 with a few gaps, and the stalks are 3 to 6 tall: plant them and trim the tops with an axe to keep them short. That is 229 bamboo in 52 stalks (layers 1–6).",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","Scatter 43 flowers over the grass, thicker near the bamboo: 17 poppies, 12 dandelions and 14 oxeye daisies (layer 1).",[]],
["Add the red mushrooms","Put 12 red mushrooms on the tops of the lower log ledges round the foot of the tree, 4 on the left steps, 4 along the back and 1 on the right, and 3 more on the grass: 1 front left, 1 front right and 1 on the right (layers 1–4). They are tiny, so tuck them in where you can see them from the front.",[]],
["Finish with tall grass","Finally, put short grass in loose clumps on the bare ground, thickest by the stream and the bamboo and none on the path: 121 blocks (layer 1).",[]]]},
{id:"medieval-watchtower",name:"Medieval Watchtower",emoji:"🏰",tags:["Medieval","Defense","Survival"],diff:"Hard",time:"3 hr",size:"22×20",photos:[P9],pr:[0.824],photoCredit:"",desc:"A tall stone-brick watchtower with window slits, a corbelled crown of merlons, a stairway up to its ledge, sandstone steps, a ruined wall, grassy terraces and a couple of oaks.",steps:[["Raise the first hillside terrace","Mark an 11 × 11 square on flat ground for the tower's base, about 22 blocks across and 20 deep with everything around it. The side with the stairway and door is the front, and left and right are as you face the tower from the front. Behind and beside that square, wrap a band one block wide, touching it, round the back and 4 rows down each side: 13 blocks across the back and 4 along each side, 21 columns in all. Make each column 4 blocks of coarse dirt with a grass block on top (layers 1–5), and take one block off a few of them so the edge is uneven. That is 77 coarse dirt and 21 grass blocks.",[]],
["Add the second terrace","One block further out, wrap a second band round the back and sides: 15 across the back and 5 along each side, 25 columns. Make each 2 blocks of coarse dirt under a grass block (layers 1–3), again taking a block off a few. That is 42 coarse dirt and 25 grass blocks.",[]],
["Add the lowest terrace","One more band, one block further out: 17 across the back and 6 along each side, 29 grass blocks laid on the ground (layer 1).",[]],
["Build the stone plinth","On the marked 11 × 11 square, raise a ring of stone bricks one block thick and 6 tall (layers 1–6). It stands against the first terrace. Mix the bricks as you go, scattering mossy and cracked ones so it looks old. That is 240 blocks: 197 stone bricks, 22 mossy and 21 cracked.",[]],
["Lay the ledge and floor","Cover the whole plinth with a flat plate, 11 × 11, on layer 7. Use cobblestone for the middle 9 × 9 and stone bricks round the edge, with a few mossy ones; the edge is a one-block ledge round the tower. That is 81 cobblestone and 40 stone bricks.",[]],
["Stand the front piers","In front of the plinth, one block out from its front wall, stand 4 piers: at the 1st, 3rd, 9th and 11th blocks across the front. Make each 5 stone bricks tall, then put a stone brick stair on top (layer 6) with the high side against the wall. That is 24 blocks, layers 1–6, with 4 stairs.",[]],
["Build the front stairway","Centred between the middle two piers, 3 blocks wide, build 6 steps up to the ledge. The step furthest out, 6 blocks in front of the wall, is a row of stone brick stairs on layer 1; each step nearer the wall is one layer higher, so the last row, right in front of the wall, is on layer 6. The high side of every stair faces the tower. Fill solid stone brick under each row of stairs. That is 18 stairs and 45 bricks.",[]],
["Build the sandstone steps","Against the right wall of the plinth, build 5 columns of smooth sandstone going outward, counting rows from the back of the plinth. Column 1, touching the wall: rows 6–10, 5 tall. Column 2: rows 6–9, 4 tall. Column 3: rows 7–9, 3 tall. Column 4: rows 7–9, 2 tall. Column 5: rows 8–9, 1 tall. Top each column with smooth sandstone stairs, high side towards the plinth, on its two end rows and a few in the middle, and full blocks elsewhere. Then put a slab on top of the middle of columns 1 and 2, on row 8. That is 45 blocks, 13 stairs and 2 slabs.",[]],
["Raise the ruined wall","Out from the left wall of the plinth, on rows 9 and 10 from the back, build a wall two blocks thick in 6 columns, getting lower as it goes: 6, 5, 5, 4, 3 and 2 blocks tall (layers 1–7). Use stone bricks, mixed with mossy and cracked ones. On columns 1 to 3, add one extra brick on top of every other block, in a checkerboard, like broken battlements. Finish the three outer columns with a stone brick stair on top, facing the plinth, on alternate rows. That is 56 blocks: 3 stairs and 53 bricks.",[]],
["Shape the front terraces","At the front, make two small grassy slopes. On the right, in front of the sandstone steps, starting level with the plinth's front wall: a 5 × 5 patch whose corner next to the plinth is 5 blocks tall (layer 5) and drops one block for every block you move away from that corner, to the right or to the front. On the left, beyond the left pier, a 4 × 4 patch that rises to 4 blocks and drops the same way. Use coarse dirt under grass blocks, and take a block off a few of the higher columns. That is 40 coarse dirt and 41 grass blocks.",[]],
["Start the tower shaft","On the plate, stand a ring of stone bricks 9 wide and one thick, with a ledge one block wide all round it, 3 tall (layers 8–10). In the middle of the front wall leave a 1-block doorway, 2 tall (layers 8–9). That is 94 blocks: 88 stone bricks, 6 mossy and 0 cracked.",[]],
["Raise the first window course","Add 2 more layers of the ring (layers 11–12), but on each of the four walls leave a 1-block slit, 2 tall, at the 3rd and the 7th block of the wall. That is 8 slits. Count the wall as 9 blocks, corners included. The ring takes 48 blocks: 35 stone bricks, 10 mossy and 3 cracked.",[]],
["Glaze the first slits","Put a glass pane in each of the 8 slits, 2 tall (layers 11–12). That is 16 panes.",[]],
["Carry the shaft up","Raise the plain ring 3 more layers (layers 13–15), with no gaps. That is 96 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the second window course","Add 2 more layers (layers 16–17) with the same 8 slits, directly above the first ones. The ring takes 48 blocks.",[]],
["Glaze the second slits","Glass panes again in the 8 new slits, 2 tall (layers 16–17). That is 16 panes.",[]],
["Finish the shaft","Raise the plain ring 2 more layers (layers 18–19). That is 64 blocks.",[]],
["Hang the door","Stack 2 spruce planks in the doorway for a door (layers 8–9). That is 2 planks. You reach it from the ledge at the top of the stairway.",[]],
["Build the corbels","On layer 20, run one more course of the 9-wide ring of stone bricks (32 blocks). Round it, one block out on every side, place a ring of 40 stone brick stairs, 11 × 11, with the high side against the tower, so they stick out like brackets. At the four corners the stairs will join their neighbours into an outer corner.",[]],
["Lay the lookout deck","On layer 21, put a ring of stone bricks round the 11 × 11 edge, on top of the stairs (40 blocks), and fill the 9 × 9 inside with spruce planks (81 planks).",[]],
["Raise the merlons","On the edge of the deck, build the battlements, 2 blocks tall (layers 22–23). Going along each side of 11 blocks from a corner: two blocks up, one gap, two up, one gap, two up, one gap, then two up. The gaps fall on the 3rd, 6th and 9th blocks of every side, and each corner is a merlon. That is 56 blocks: 49 stone bricks, 4 mossy and 3 cracked.",[]],
["Light the battlements","Put a torch on top of each of the 4 corner merlons (layer 24). That is 4 torches.",[]],
["Light the ledge","Stand a lantern on the ledge on each of its 4 corners, and one either side of the doorstep, 1 block each side of the doorway, in front of the wall (layer 8). That is 6 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant two oak trees","On the lowest terrace, in line with the back wall of the plinth: 8 blocks to the right of the middle, stack 6 oak logs on the grass block (layers 2–7), and 8 blocks to the left, stack 5 (layers 2–6). That is 11 logs.",[]],
["Crown the trees","Around the top two logs of each trunk, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves without its corners, with the trunk in the middle. Then put a 3 × 3 square on top, and a plus of 5 leaves on top of that. Where a leaf would land on a terrace block, leave it out. That is 105 leaves, layers 5–9.",[]],
["Finish the terraces","Last, on top of the grass blocks of the terraces, plant 38 short grass, 2 poppies and 4 dandelions in loose clumps, and leave the rest bare (layers 2–6). That is 44 blocks.",[]]]},
{id:"cherry-pagoda",name:"Cherry Blossom Pagoda",emoji:"🌸",tags:["Fantasy","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"7 hr",size:"29×29",photos:[P7],pr:[0.888],photoCredit:"",desc:"A four-tier pagoda on a pond island: tiled dark roofs with upturned pink corners, cherry-wood walls, cream balconies, a ringed spire, a stone causeway and four stepped cherry trees.",steps:[["Lay the moss islands","On flat ground, mark out a square 29 blocks across and find the middle block. In each of the four corners, lay a patch of moss blocks on layer 1, about 5 blocks across and centred 11 blocks out from the middle both ways; these are the tree islands. Let each patch spread a little towards the middle of the corner so the edge looks ragged. Then add a landing in the middle of the front edge, 5 wide and 2 deep, 11 and 12 blocks out from the middle. That is 142 moss blocks.",[]],
["Fill the pond","Between the islands, fill the ground with water on layer 1: a rounded square 21 blocks across, 10 blocks out from the middle each way, with the corners cut off where it meets the islands. Keep clear a 13 × 13 square in the middle for the plinth, a strip 3 wide in front of it for the causeway, and four single blocks for lamp posts, 2 blocks either side of the middle of that strip, 8 and 10 blocks out from the middle. That is 216 water blocks.",[]],
["Lay the plinth","On layer 1 in the middle, lay stone bricks 11 wide and 11 deep, and then wrap them in a ring of stone brick slabs, bottom half, to make a plinth 13 across. In the middle of the front edge, three of the ring blocks are full stone bricks instead of slabs: that is where the steps will go. That is 124 bricks and 45 slabs.",[]],
["Lay the causeway","From the front edge of the plinth, lay a path of stone bricks 3 wide and 4 long straight out across the water to the landing, on layer 1. That is 12 bricks.",[]],
["Raise the lamp posts","Beside the causeway, in the four gaps you left in the water, build a lamp post on each: 2 stone bricks (layers 1–2) with a lantern on top (layer 3). Two stand either side of the path 8 blocks out from the middle, and two 10 blocks out. That is 8 bricks and 4 lanterns.",[]],
["Lay the ground floor","On the plinth, lay cherry planks 11 wide and 11 deep on layer 2, right on top of the stone bricks. This is the floor of the ground-floor hall, and it leaves a ledge of slabs all round. That is 121 planks.",[]],
["Add the door steps","On layer 2, on top of the three full bricks at the middle of the front edge, put stone brick stairs with the high side facing the house. They lift you up from the causeway to the floor. That is 3 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the ground-floor pillars","The walls are a square 9 blocks across, one block in from the edge of the floor, held up by pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 3–7). Along each side the pattern is: a corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner pillar. The gap of three in the middle of the front is the door. That is 12 pillars, 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall in the hall","Fill the gaps between the pillars with cherry planks. On the bottom row (layer 3) and on layer 5, fill every gap except the three-wide door in the front. Leave layer 4 empty for the windows. Then fill every gap on layers 6–7, including above the door, which makes it a doorway 3 wide and 3 tall. That is 74 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the windows","On layer 4, fill every empty gap in the walls with dark oak planks to make dark shuttered windows, except the open door. If you would rather see inside, use glass panes here instead. That is 17 planks.",[]],
["Furnish the shrine","Inside, lay a strip of red carpet 1 wide and 5 long on layer 3, starting just inside the door and running towards the back. Leave one block, then build an altar of 3 stone bricks across the back, 3 blocks behind the middle (layer 3). Put a lantern on each end of the altar and a flower pot in the middle on layer 4. Finally stand a lantern on the floor in each of the four inner corners, 3 blocks from the middle both ways. That is 5 carpets, 3 bricks, 6 lanterns and 1 flower pot.",[]],
["Cap the ground floor","On layer 8, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the balcony floor for the next level, and the roof is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the ground floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 8, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 8, and on top of them (layer 9) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle. That is 68 slabs, 48 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 8: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 9. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 7, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Fence the second-floor balcony","Put a ring of birch fences on layer 9, on the outer plank ring of the balcony, 11 blocks across, just inside the tile ring. It will run round the foot of the walls above. That is 40 fences.",[]],
["Stand the second-floor pillars","On the inner plank ring of the balcony, the one 9 blocks across, stand the pillars again, in the same pattern as the floor below: 12 pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 9–13). A corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner. That is 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall the second floor","Fill every gap between the pillars with cherry planks on the bottom row (layer 9) and on the top three rows, but leave the layer in between empty for the windows. There is no door up here, so all 20 gaps are filled. That is 80 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the second-floor windows","On layer 10, fill the 20 empty gaps with dark oak planks, as on the floor below. That is 20 planks.",[]],
["Cap the second floor","On layer 14, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the balcony floor for the next level, and the roof is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the second floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 14, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 14, and on top of them (layer 15) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle. That is 68 slabs, 48 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the second-floor corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 14: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 15. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the second-floor eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 13, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Fence the third-floor balcony","Put a ring of birch fences on layer 15, on the outer plank ring of the balcony, 11 blocks across, just inside the tile ring. It will run round the foot of the walls above. That is 40 fences.",[]],
["Stand the third-floor pillars","On the inner plank ring of the balcony, the one 9 blocks across, stand the pillars again, in the same pattern as the floor below: 12 pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 15–19). A corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner. That is 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall the third floor","Fill every gap between the pillars with cherry planks on the bottom row (layer 15) and on the top three rows, but leave the layer in between empty for the windows. There is no door up here, so all 20 gaps are filled. That is 80 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the third-floor windows","On layer 16, fill the 20 empty gaps with dark oak planks, as on the floor below. That is 20 planks.",[]],
["Cap the third floor","On layer 20, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the balcony floor for the next level, and the roof is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the third floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 20, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 20, and on top of them (layer 21) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle. That is 68 slabs, 48 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the third-floor corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 20: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 21. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the third-floor eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 19, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Fence the top-floor balcony","Put a ring of birch fences on layer 21, on the outer plank ring of the balcony, 11 blocks across, just inside the tile ring. It will run round the foot of the walls above. That is 40 fences.",[]],
["Stand the top-floor pillars","On the inner plank ring of the balcony, the one 9 blocks across, stand the pillars again, in the same pattern as the floor below: 12 pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 21–25). A corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner. That is 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall the top floor","Fill every gap between the pillars with cherry planks on the bottom row (layer 21) and on the top three rows, but leave the layer in between empty for the windows. There is no door up here, so all 20 gaps are filled. That is 80 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the top-floor windows","On layer 22, fill the 20 empty gaps with dark oak planks, as on the floor below. That is 20 planks.",[]],
["Cap the top floor","On layer 26, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the foot of the pyramid roof, which is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the top floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 26, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 26, and on top of them (layer 27) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle; the four corner blocks of the tile ring are left empty for the ridge. That is 68 slabs, 44 planks and 20 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the top-floor corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 26: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 27. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the top-floor eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 25, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Raise the pyramid","Carry the top roof up as a steep pyramid of stair rings. The 11-across and 9-across rings sit on planks on layer 27, with tiles on layer 28 (stairs and slabs alternating). The 7-across and 5-across rings sit on planks on layer 28, with plain stairs on layer 29. The 3-across ring has planks on layer 29 and stairs on layer 30, and the middle block gets planks on layers 29 and 30. Every stair has its high side to the middle, and the four corner blocks of every ring stay empty for the ridges. That is 102 planks, 36 slabs and 64 stairs.",[]],
["Run the pink ridges","Down each corner of the roof, climb a ridge of cherry wood, one step for each ring: a cherry plank with a cherry stair on top, on the empty corner block of each ring from the 13-across ring (stair on layer 27) up to the 3-across ring (stair on layer 30). Face every stair towards the middle. Six steps a corner, four corners. That is 24 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Crown it with the spire","On the middle block at layer 31, stack 10 dark oak logs (layers 31–40) for the pole. Thread dark oak slabs, bottom half, round it like discs: a full ring of 8 around the pole on layers 32 and 34, and four in a plus sign on layers 36, 38 and 40. Finish with a dark oak plank on layer 41 and a lightning rod on layer 42. That is 28 slabs, 1 plank and 1 rod.",[]],
["Light the lanterns","Hang a lantern under the cherry plank on each corner of all four eaves (one block below the eave, on layers 7, 13, 19 and 25): 16 in all. Then stand a lantern on the corner post of each of the three balcony rails (layers 10, 16 and 22): 12 more. That is 28 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the four cherry trees","On each island, in the middle of the patch, stand a trunk of 4 cherry logs (layers 2–5). The two in front get big stepped crowns of cherry leaves: 7 × 7 with the corners cut off on layer 6, 5 × 5 on layer 7, 3 × 3 on layer 8 and one on top at layer 9. The two at the back are smaller: 5 × 5 on layer 6, 3 × 3 on layer 7 and one on top at layer 8. Leave a few gaps in each, but make sure every leaf touches another. That is 16 logs and 207 leaves.",[]],
["Scatter the petals","Finish by scattering pink petals on the moss islands, on layer 2, on roughly one block in three. That is 55 petals.",[]]]},
{id:"starter-farm",name:"Starter Farm",emoji:"🌾",tags:["Farm","Survival","Starter"],diff:"Easy",time:"45 min",size:"17×18",photos:[P8],pr:[1.761],photoCredit:"",desc:"A round-cornered wheat field ringed with mossy cobblestone and lanterns, with a pumpkin-head scarecrow, a fence gate, leafy hedges and wildflowers around it.",steps:[["Mark the field and place the water","Find flat ground about 17 blocks wide and 18 deep; the finished farm, with its hedge, trees and flowers, takes about that much. In the middle, mark out a 9 × 9 square for the wheat field. The side with the gate is the front, and left and right are as you face the field from the gate. Dig out the one block in the exact middle, the 5th block of the 5th row, and fill it with water (layer 1). One water block keeps farmland up to 4 blocks away moist, so it reaches the whole field.",[]],
["Till the farmland","Hoe the ground inside the 9 × 9 square into farmland (layer 1), leaving out three kinds of block: the water in the middle, the four corner blocks of the square, which stay as plain ground so the field has cut corners, and the 5th block of the 2nd row from the back, which stays bare for the scarecrow. That is 75 farmland blocks. Counted from the back, rows 1 and 9 are 7 blocks wide and rows 2 to 8 are 9 wide, except that row 2 has the gap.",[]],
["Plant the wheat","Plant wheat on every farmland block (layer 2) except the 4th and 6th blocks of row 2 from the back, the two blocks either side of the scarecrow's gap. They stay bare for its arms. That is 73 wheat. Give the field a little time to grow if you want it fully golden.",[]],
["Lay the ring base","Wrap a ring one block thick around the whole field, touching it on every side, in mixed cobblestone and mossy cobblestone (layer 1). Each side has 7 blocks between the corners, and each corner is a little L of 3 blocks: the block on the diagonal and one beside it each way. Leave a 3-block gap in the front side, the 2nd to 4th of its 7 blocks counting from the left; that is the gate. That is 37 blocks: 23 cobblestone and 14 mossy cobblestone. Scatter the mossy ones so the ring looks weathered.",[]],
["Raise the corners and some of the sides","On layer 2, put a full block on top of every corner block (all 12) and on some of the side blocks. On each side, count the 7 blocks between the corners (on the front, from the left). On the back side, the 2nd, 4th and 6th; on the right side, the 2nd, 4th and 6th; on the left side, the 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th; on the front side, only the 7th. Mix mossy cobblestone and cobblestone. That is 23 blocks: 14 mossy and 9 plain.",[]],
["Fill the gaps with slabs","On each side block that did not get a full block, place a slab on layer 2, bottom half, so the ring dips and rises: the 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th on the back and right sides, the 2nd, 4th and 6th on the left side, and the 6th on the front side. That is 12 slabs: 5 mossy and 7 cobblestone.",[]],
["Step up the corners","On layer 3, put a mossy cobblestone block on top of each corner's diagonal block, then a mossy cobblestone stair on each of the two blocks beside it, with the high side against the new block, so every corner climbs to a little peak. That is 4 blocks and 8 stairs.",[]],
["Raise the gate posts","The two front blocks either side of the gap, the 1st and the 5th of the front side, are the gate posts. Stack two more blocks on each (layers 2–3), mixing cobblestone and mossy cobblestone. That is 4 blocks: 2 cobblestone and 2 mossy.",[]],
["Lay the path","Cover the ground through the gap and in front of it with coarse dirt: the 3 blocks of the gap itself, then 5 blocks across the next row, from just outside one gate post to just outside the other, then 3 blocks across the row after that, lined up with the gap (layer 1). That is 11 blocks.",[]],
["Close the gate","Put a spruce fence in each of the 3 blocks of the gap, on top of the coarse dirt (layer 2). They join onto the gate posts either side. That is 3 fences. If you want a working gate, swap the middle fence for a fence gate.",[]],
["Scatter the boulders","Just outside the ring, touching it or a block away, set 12 single blocks of mossy cobblestone on the ground as boulders: 3 along each of the right, left, back and front. Put a mossy cobblestone slab on top of 3 of them, one each on the right, left and back, so they are two layers tall. That is 15 blocks, layers 1–2. Keep the path clear.",[]],
["Light the ring","Stand a lantern on top of each of the 4 corner peaks and each of the 2 gate posts (that is 6 on layer 4), and on the middle block of the back side and the middle block of the right side (2 on layer 3). That is 8 lanterns.",[]],
["Start the scarecrow","In the bare block in the 2nd row from the back, set a coarse dirt block, then stack a spruce fence on it and a cobblestone block on that (layers 1–3). The fence is the post and the cobblestone is the body. That is 3 blocks.",[]],
["Give it arms","Beside the post and the body, on each side, stack 2 spruce fences (layers 2–3). The lower fence stands on the farmland you left bare and joins the post; the upper one joins the body. That is 4 fences.",[]],
["Add the pumpkin head","On top of the body, on layer 4, set a jack o'lantern with its face turned to the front, towards the gate. That is 1 block.",[]],
["Top it with a hat","On layer 5, put a spruce plank on top of the head, a spruce stair on each side of it with the high side against the plank, and a spruce slab beyond each stair, bottom half, to make a wide brim. Then put one more spruce slab on top of the plank, on layer 6, for the crown. That is 1 plank, 2 stairs and 3 slabs.",[]],
["Grow the back hedge","Leave a one-block gap behind the back of the ring, where the boulders sit, and behind it build a hedge two blocks thick and 15 wide, centred on the field, out of oak leaves. In the middle, 11 blocks wide, make it 2 or 3 blocks tall, about half of each; for the last 2 blocks at each end, make it 1 or 2 tall. Leave the 2nd block in from each end of the back row empty for a tree. Layer 1 takes 28 leaves, layer 2 takes 25 and layer 3 takes 12, 65 in all.",[]],
["Plant two tree trunks","In each of the two gaps, stack oak logs 5 high (layers 1–5). That is 10 logs. They stand in the back row of the hedge, so the hedge wraps round them.",[]],
["Crown the trees","Around the top of each trunk, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves on layers 4 and 5, leaving off the four corners and keeping the trunk in the middle, then a 3 × 3 square on layer 6. That is 98 leaves for both trees, 40 on layer 4, 40 on layer 5 and 18 on layer 6.",[]],
["Add two leafy bushes","On the ground a little way out from each side of the field, set a bush of oak leaves 2 wide and 3 deep: the left one 7 and 8 blocks out from the middle, beside the left boulders, and the right one the same on the right, both lined up with the 2nd to 4th rows of the field from the back. Cover each with 6 leaves on layer 1 and then about 4 or 5 more on layer 2, leaving a few gaps. That is 12 leaves on layer 1 and 9 on layer 2, 21 in all.",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","On bare grass around the build, outside the ring and the boulders, plant 25 flowers in loose groups: 8 poppies, 6 oxeye daisies, 5 dandelions, 4 pink tulips and 2 alliums. Keep the path and the front of the gate clear.",[]],
["Finish with short grass","Finally, fill in a few gaps between the flowers with 9 blocks of short grass, mostly near the ring and the hedge, so the ground looks wild rather than mown.",[]]]},
{id:"mushroom-house",name:"Mushroom House",emoji:"🍄",tags:["Fantasy","Cozy","Nature"],diff:"Medium",time:"1 hr",size:"20×18",photos:[P4],pr:[1.107],photoCredit:"",desc:"A fairy-tale mushroom cottage: a five-tier spotted red cap over an uneven white stem, a dark oak awning over the door, a winding path, poppy beds and a little toadstool on a grassy terrace.",steps:[["Build the grassy steps","Find open ground about 20 blocks wide and 18 deep. The side with the door is the front, and left and right are as you face it from the front. Mark where the stem will stand, a 7 × 7 square, and to the right of it, about 4 blocks away and level with its back 5 rows, build three grassy steps, each column 5 blocks long front to back. The column nearest the stem is 3 tall: 2 coarse dirt with a grass block on top. The middle column is 2 tall: 1 coarse dirt and a grass block. The outer column is a single grass block (layers 1–3). That is 15 coarse dirt and 15 grass blocks.",[]],
["Lay the winding path","Dirt path blocks sit level with the grass, so put them in place of the grass blocks on layer 1. Start at the doorstep, the block in front of the left block of the stem's front face, and lay 2 blocks straight out from the door, then a row of 3 in front of those, centred on the same line. Then wander off to the front left in a zigzag: a row of 2 blocks, each row one block further forward and one block further left than the last, for 4 more rows. That is 13 dirt path blocks.",[]],
["Start the stem","Mark the stem: a 7 × 7 square with the corners cut off, three blocks from each corner (the corner block and the two beside it). That leaves 37 blocks. Fill layers 1–2 with white concrete, leaving the door spot empty on layer 2: that is the left block of the 3-block front face. That is 73 blocks.",[]],
["Carry the stem up","Do the same on layers 3–4, again leaving the door spot empty on layer 3. That is 73 blocks.",[]],
["Make the stem uneven","Now raise each column to its own height, with layers 5 and up. The numbers are how many blocks tall each column is in total, counting the layers you have already placed, with a dot where there is no block. Columns marked 4 are already finished. Look at it from the front, rows from the back to the front, left to right. row 1: · · 6 5 4 · ·; row 2: · 6 8 6 6 8 ·; row 3: 5 8 8 8 8 6 5; row 4: 4 8 8 8 8 7 6; row 5: 5 6 8 8 8 8 5; row 6: · 8 6 8 8 6 ·; row 7: · · 4 4 6 · ·. The 17 columns marked 8 reach layer 8 and carry the cap. That is 96 more blocks, layers 5–8.",[]],
["Hang the door","In the door spot, stack 2 dark oak planks (layers 2–3). That is 2 planks. It is the left block of the front face, which is 4 blocks tall.",[]],
["Set the awning posts","In the second row in front of the stem's front face, put a post on each side of the line of the door: one to the left of it and one to the right. Make each 3 tall out of dark oak fence (layers 1–3). That is 6 fences.",[]],
["Roof the awning","On layer 4, in the first row in front of the stem, lay 3 dark oak planks across, from the post on the left to the post on the right, then a dark oak slab, bottom half, at each end. In the second row, lay a row of 5 slabs over the posts, one block wider than the planks on each side. Finish with one more plank on top of the middle plank, on layer 5. That is 4 planks and 7 slabs.",[]],
["Hang a lantern","Hang a lantern from the middle slab between the posts, on layer 3. That is 1 lantern.",[]],
["Lay the first cap tier","The cap starts on layer 9, on top of the stem. Lay a red mushroom block square 11 × 11, centred on the stem so it sticks out 2 blocks on every side, with three blocks cut off each corner, as you did the stem. That is 109 blocks.",[]],
["Second tier","On layer 10, lay a 9 × 9 square, centred, with three blocks cut off each corner. That is 69 blocks.",[]],
["Third tier","On layer 11, lay a 7 × 7 square with just the one corner block cut off at each corner. That is 45 blocks.",[]],
["Fourth tier","On layer 12, lay a 5 × 5 square with the four corner blocks cut off. That is 21 blocks.",[]],
["Top of the cap","On layer 13, finish with a block 5 wide and 3 deep, centred. That is 15 blocks, so the cap is 5 layers deep.",[]],
["Grow a bush","Behind the stem and to the right, 3 to 5 blocks out from its right edge and 1 to 3 rows behind its back, build a bush of oak leaves 3 × 3: 9 leaves on layer 1 and 6 on layer 2, leaving a few gaps. That is 15 leaves.",[]],
["Plant an oak tree","To the left of the stem, 5 blocks out from its left edge and level with the 3rd row from the back, stack 7 oak logs (layers 1–7).",[]],
["Crown the tree","Around the top 2 logs, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves without the corners (layers 6–7), then a 3 × 3 square on layer 8, then a plus of 5 leaves on layer 9. The trunk fills the middle of the first three. That is 54 leaves.",[]],
["Add a little toadstool","On the highest grass block of the steps, in the middle of its 5 rows, put a mushroom stem block and then a red mushroom block on top (layers 4–5). That is 1 stem and 1 cap.",[]],
["Plant the poppies","Plant 21 poppies in two beds: 12 in a patch to the right of the door, in front of the right side of the stem, 3 to 6 blocks out from the line of the door; 8 in a patch to the left of the path; and 1 in front of the stem, near the path. That is 21 poppies (layer 1).",[]],
["Scatter some daisies","Scatter 5 oxeye daisies among the poppies and along the path (layer 1).",[]],
["Finish with tall grass","Finally, put short grass in loose clumps on the bare ground all round, and on the grassy steps: 66 blocks, thickest near the beds and the edges, and none right against the stem (layers 1–4).",[]]]}
];
